Finding: {"title":"Even if activated, setTenantContext's session GUC cannot be safely used with the shared connection Pool","severity":"HIGH","area":"Multi-tenant data isolation / connection pooling","files":["src/lib/db.ts","src/lib/db-tenant-context.ts"],"detail":"db (src/lib/db.ts:59) is a lazy Drizzle client over a shared @neondatabase/serverless Pool (max 8 connections). setTenantContext calls db.execute(sql`SELECT set_config(..., true)`) — set_config with is_local=true is transaction-scoped and reverts on whichever physical connection serviced that call. Drizzle's pooled db.execute checks out an arbitrary connection per call; there's no pool.connect() + pinned client per logical request/transaction here. Even if setTenantContext were called, the next query for the same 'session' could land on a different pooled connection with no tenant context set, or inherit stale context left by a prior request that was never cleared.","impact":"RLS-via-session-GUC as currently structured is fundamentally unsafe under connection pooling: it can silently apply the wrong tenant's context to a query, or none at all. This is a correctness bug waiting to be exploited the moment someone 'fixes' the dead-code issue above by calling setTenantContext from middleware without also re-architecting connection handling.","recommendation":"If RLS is to be the real enforcement mechanism, tenant context must be set on a per-transaction basis using a pinned client (pool.connect() -> set_config -> run all tenant queries on that client -> release), or set at the start of every db.transaction() callback, never on the ambient pooled db. Otherwise keep RLS as documentation-only and rely solely on the application-level flightSchoolId checks that are actually doing the work today.","confidence":"medium"}
